<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have become the foundation of modern AI agents in applications such as coding, web search, and medicine. As agent execution increasingly relies on frequent LLM invocations, inference efficiency has become a critical factor in both system throughput and user experience. Consequently, extensive research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> has explored various techniques to achieve extreme optimization on LLM inference, encompassing algorithm optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. (2026)</a></cite>, system enhancements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dao et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and hardware advancements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moon et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prabhakar et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Speculative decoding has emerged as a mainstream inference acceleration technique, which has been widely adopted by prominent LLMs (<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, DeepSeek-V4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2026)</a></cite>) and deployed in real-world serving systems(<span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, SGLang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2024)</a></cite> and vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. (2023)</a></cite>). A lightweight draft model first generates multiple candidate tokens, which are then verified by the target model in a single forward pass. By accepting multiple tokens at once, speculative decoding can substantially improve inference efficiency without compromising accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, agentic workloads differ significantly from conventional conversational inference. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), agent execution typically involves long inputs, short outputs, frequent multi-turn interactions, and continuously growing contexts. Under such workloads, we observe two major limitations of existing speculative decoding methods. <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) From the perspective of algorithmic performance</span>, Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b) shows that the methods that achieve high acceptance lengths on conventional benchmarks can drop to only <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\sim 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\sim 2</annotation></semantics></math> accepted tokens on agentic workloads, corresponding to a degradation of more than <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Lightweight draft models increasingly struggle to independently model the long historical context required to match the predictive distribution of the target model. <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) From the perspective of system efficiency</span>, maintaining the draft model’s historical states introduces non-negligible system overhead. Existing speculative drafters must continuously update their KV cache with newly accepted tokens and maintain historical states for subsequent generation and prefix reuse. Under frequent multi-turn agent execution, these operations can account for <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%\sim 20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%\sim 20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of end-to-end inference latency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These two limitations share a common source: the draft model is required to independently encode and maintain an increasingly long history, though the target model has processed the same context. More importantly, this growing history continuously increases the explicit context length and positional range faced by the lightweight drafter, making the prediction problem increasingly difficult. We therefore adopt a different design principle: <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rather than reconstructing the full history inside the draft model, the drafter directly reuses contextual representations produced by the target model and performs only local speculative prediction around the current decoding position.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this insight, we propose <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>, a history-free speculative decoding framework for agentic AI. <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> employs a history-free parallel backbone that directly consumes target hidden states and performs local prediction around the current decoding position without maintaining historical draft KV cache. A lightweight sequential head restores the missing intra-block causal dependency directly in the hidden space, avoiding expensive intermediate vocabulary projections. Finally, we introduce a two-stage training strategy that first bridges the structural mismatch using an isomorphic history-aware teacher and then directly aligns <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> with the target model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We train <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> for Qwen3-4B, Qwen3-8B models and evaluate it on both general-purpose, long-context and agentic workloads. <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> achieves <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\sim 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\sim 6</annotation></semantics></math> acceptance length and improves over DSpark by up to <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>, completely eliminating the draft model’s historical KV-cache maintenance overhead.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23787v1/agent.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/163;" width="537" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) Agentic workloads characteristics. (b) Speculative decoding performance loss.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Speculative Decoding</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Speculative decoding accelerates LLM inference through a draft-and-verify pipeline shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Speculative Decoding ‣ 2 Background ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a). Given the current context, a lightweight draft model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{D}</annotation></semantics></math> generates <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> candidate tokens, which are subsequently verified in parallel by the target model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> tokens are produced per verification round, the practical speedup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA (2026)</a></cite> can be approximately expressed as</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Speedup}=\frac{\mathrm{Time}_{M_{T}}(1)\times\tau}{\mathrm{Time}_{M_{T}}(N)+\mathrm{Time}_{M_{D}}(N)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Speedup</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Time</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Time</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Time</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Speedup}=\frac{\mathrm{Time}_{M_{T}}(1)\times\tau}{\mathrm{Time}_{M_{T}}(N)+\mathrm{Time}_{M_{D}}(N)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, speculative decoding performance mainly depends on two factors: acceptance length <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and draft-model latency <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Time}_{M_{D}}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Time</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Time}_{M_{D}}(N)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Existing draft models mainly follow three architectural paradigms, summarized in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Speculative Decoding ‣ 2 Background ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b)(c)(d). Autoregressive drafters, represented by EAGLE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2026)</a></cite>, explicitly preserve causal dependencies among speculative tokens but require sequential draft steps. Parallel drafters, such as DFlash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2026)</a></cite>, predict all speculative positions in one forward pass, substantially reducing drafting latency but removing intra-block causal dependency. Semi-autoregressive drafters, including DSpark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. (2026)</a></cite> and Domino <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2026)</a></cite>, combine a parallel backbone with a lightweight sequential module to balance drafting latency and prediction quality. Despite their different structures, these designs share one property: the draft model still explicitly depends on the historical context, whose length grows with decoding. In contrast, Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Speculative Decoding ‣ 2 Background ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(e) shows that <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> removes this growing historical dependency by directly reusing contextual features produced by the target model and performs local prediction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.23787v1/background2.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/241;" width="537" height="241"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Speculative decoding pipeline and comparison of existing draft architectures with <em id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Model Distillation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training a speculative draft model can be viewed as knowledge distillation from the target model. Different architectures adopt different supervision objectives. EAGLE performs distribution-level distillation using soft-target cross-entropy (CE), which is equivalent to minimizing forward Kullback-Leibler divergence up to a constant. DFlash primarily adopts token-level CE, while DSpark combines CE with total variation (TV) distance to improve distribution overlap between the draft and target models. Domino applies token-level supervision to both parallel and causally corrected predictions through a training curriculum. These differences are particularly relevant to <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> because its history-free prediction structure differs substantially from the autoregressive target model, making direct distribution-level distillation non-trivial.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Motivation</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Two Important Observations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic workloads expose two limitations of existing speculative decoding methods: degraded acceptance length and increasing historical-state maintenance overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Degraded Acceptance Length.</span> As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b), existing speculative decoding methods achieve high acceptance lengths on general workloads such as GSM8K and MBPP, but degrade substantially on agentic workloads such as SWE-Bench and AgentLongBench. As the context grows, lightweight draft models increasingly struggle to capture the information required to match the target model’s predictions, causing acceptance lengths to drop to only <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\sim 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\sim 2</annotation></semantics></math> tokens in some workloads.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">KV Cache Maintenance.</span> Existing speculative drafters must maintain historical KV states for subsequent generation. After each target-model verification, accepted tokens are committed to the draft model to update its KV cache, while prefix caching further requires historical draft states to be preserved across agent turns. Under frequent multi-turn workloads with continuously growing contexts, these operations become increasingly expensive. Using SGLang to evaluate the multi-token prediction module in DeepSeek-V4, we observe that the KV cache updating and maintenance can account for <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%\sim 20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%\sim 20\%</annotation></semantics></math> of end-to-end inference latency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Analysis and Findings</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the above problems appear at different levels, they share a common cause: the draft model is required to independently process and maintain the growing historical context shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Speculative Decoding ‣ 2 Background ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. From the model perspective, a lightweight drafter has only a fraction of the target model’s capacity, making it increasingly difficult to reproduce the target model’s long-context representation as history grows. From the system perspective, preserving the same history requires maintaining and repeatedly updating an increasingly large draft KV cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The key inefficiency is therefore that the draft model repeatedly reconstructs historical information that has already been processed by the target model. We instead argue that the drafter should directly reuse the target model’s contextual representations and dedicate its limited capacity to modeling the information required for local speculative prediction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span><em id="S4.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> Framework</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the observations in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Motivation ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we design <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> to decouple historical-context modeling from speculative generation. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Speculative Decoding ‣ 2 Background ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(e), instead of requiring a lightweight draft model to repeatedly process and maintain an ever-growing history, <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> delegates global-context modeling to the target model and performs drafting only over compact target hidden states. This design transforms speculative drafting <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">from an increasingly complex growing-history modeling problem into a local prediction problem</span> whose structure and positional range remain independent of the accumulated sequence length. <em id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> realizes this principle through a history-free semi-autoregressive architecture and a two-stage training strategy.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>History-free semi-autoregressive Architecture</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 History-free semi-autoregressive Architecture ‣ 4 DLite Framework ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the inference dataflow of <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>. The target model first processes the input context and maintains its original KV cache. For each speculative round, <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> takes contextual hidden states from the target model, the current anchor token, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> draft placeholders as input. A history-free parallel backbone produces hidden representations for all speculative positions simultaneously, after which a lightweight sequential head restores the causal dependency among tokens within the speculative block. The resulting candidate tokens are finally verified by the target model following the standard speculative decoding procedure. It is worth noting that <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> maintains no historical draft KV cache throughout this process. Each speculative round is independently reconstructed from the latest target representations and the current anchor token, allowing the drafter to perform prediction locally without explicitly processing the accumulated history.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.23787v1/overview2.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/233;" width="537" height="233"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The inference dataflow of <em id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>History-free Parallel Backbone</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key idea of the history-free parallel backbone is to <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">transform speculative drafting from growing-history modeling into local prediction</span>. To this end, the backbone combines compact target hidden states with explicit local token information and performs local prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target hidden states.</span> <em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> directly reuses hidden states produced by the target model as compact contextual features. Since target hidden states and ordinary token embeddings correspond to different input sources, we introduce a learnable depth embedding to distinguish them. When hidden states from multiple target layers are used, the same embedding also identifies their source layers. For a target hidden state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from layer <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}^{(l)}=h_{t}^{(l)}+e_{\mathrm{depth}}^{(l)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>depth</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}^{(l)}=h_{t}^{(l)}+e_{\mathrm{depth}}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\mathrm{depth}}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>depth</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\mathrm{depth}}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable source-specific embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local prediction.</span> Conventional drafters operate over an ever-growing history, such that both the explicit context and global positions increase as decoding proceeds. <em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> instead delegates long-context modeling to the target model and performs draft prediction only around the current decoding position. The backbone takes the current anchor token and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> speculative positions, while using local rather than globally accumulated positions. Historical information is retained through the target hidden states, allowing the drafter to focus on local prediction without explicitly reconstructing the history. <em id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> therefore transforms growing-history modeling into a local prediction problem.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Lightweight Sequential Head</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the parallel backbone efficiently produces representations for multiple speculative positions, parallel computation removes the autoregressive dependency among tokens within the speculative block. <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> therefore introduces a lightweight sequential head that <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">restores only the missing intra-block dependency</span>, rather than performing another full contextual modeling process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> denote the starting position of a speculative block and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the intra-block offset. The target model predicts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+T}</annotation></semantics></math> autoregressively according to</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{T}\left(x_{t+T}\mid x_{&lt;t+T}\right)=p_{T}\left(x_{t+T}\mid x_{&lt;t},x_{t:t+T-1}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}\left(x_{t+T}\mid x_{&lt;t+T}\right)=p_{T}\left(x_{t+T}\mid x_{&lt;t},x_{t:t+T-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The parallel backbone, however, produces <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> before the tokens at earlier speculative positions are sampled. Since the preceding context <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math> has already been processed by the target model and conveyed through its contextual representations into <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, the information missing from parallel drafting is precisely the intra-block prefix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{t:t+T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t:t+T-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This decomposition suggests that the sequential module does not need to reconstruct the complete historical context. It only needs to encode tokens generated earlier within the current speculative block.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To recover this missing dependency efficiently, we employ a low-rank RNN-style sequential head. It recursively compresses previously generated draft tokens into a recurrent state</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T}=g_{\phi}\left(s_{T-1},E(x_{t+T-1})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T}=g_{\phi}\left(s_{T-1},E(x_{t+T-1})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the token embedding. The prediction at speculative position <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is modeled as</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{\theta,\phi}\left(x_{t+T}\mid h_{T},s_{T}\right)\approx p_{T}\left(x_{t+T}\mid x_{&lt;t},x_{t:t+T-1}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\theta,\phi}\left(x_{t+T}\mid h_{T},s_{T}\right)\approx p_{T}\left(x_{t+T}\mid x_{&lt;t},x_{t:t+T-1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> supplies the contextual information already encoded by the target model, while <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> summarizes the missing intra-block prefix. Together, they recover the information required for autoregressive prediction without reconstructing the full history inside the draft model. This preserves most contextual computation in the parallel backbone while restricting sequential execution to a lightweight low-rank module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, unlike existing semi-autoregressive methods that first project every backbone representation into full-vocabulary logits and subsequently refine these predictions with an additional sequential module, <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> performs sequential modeling directly in the hidden space. The vocabulary projection is applied only after the intra-block dependency has been incorporated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For hidden dimension <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and vocabulary size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, this avoids repeatedly applying an intermediate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\times V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\times V</annotation></semantics></math> projection to every speculative representation before sequential refinement. Such computation becomes particularly expensive under high-concurrency serving, where a large number of speculative hidden states are processed simultaneously.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23787v1/training.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/133;" width="548" height="133" alt="Refer to caption">
