<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Two-stage Training Strategy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of a speculative draft model is to approximate the predictive distribution of the target model, since a larger distribution overlap generally leads to a higher speculative acceptance probability. Distribution-level objectives such as KL divergence are therefore natural choices for distillation. However, Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1.2 Lightweight Sequential Head ‣ 4.1 History-free semi-autoregressive Architecture ‣ 4 DLite Framework ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(b) shows that directly applying KL distillation from the target model to the parallel backbone in <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> performs poorly. The two models make predictions under substantially different conditions: the target model performs autoregressive prediction over the complete historical context, whereas the backbone performs parallel prediction using compressed target hidden states,
making direct distribution alignment difficult. To bridge this structural gap, we introduce a two-stage training strategy. The warm-up stage first trains <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> against an isomorphic history-aware teacher, providing a structurally matched intermediate distillation target. The stable stage subsequently removes this teacher and directly aligns <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> with the original target model. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denote the speculative block length and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> the vocabulary size. At speculative position <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the predictive distributions of the target model, history-aware teacher, and <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{T},p_{i}^{H},q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{T},p_{i}^{H},q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and denote the ground-truth token as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Warm-up Stage.</span> In the warm-up stage, we first train an isomorphic history-aware teacher by standard token-level cross-entropy (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum_{i}w_{i}\ \mathrm{log}\ {p_{i}^{H}(y_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum_{i}w_{i}\ \mathrm{log}\ {p_{i}^{H}(y_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>). The teacher shares the same backbone and prediction structure as <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>, but retains access to historical KV cache.
Once the history-aware teacher is sufficiently trained, we use it as an intermediate distillation target for <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> using forward KL divergence:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{warmup}}=\sum_{i=1}^{K}w_{i}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(p_{i}^{H}\,\|\,q_{i}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{K}w_{i}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i}^{H}(v)\log\frac{p_{i}^{H}(v)}{q_{i}(v)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>warmup</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{warmup}}=\sum_{i=1}^{K}w_{i}D_{\mathrm{KL}}\left(p_{i}^{H}\,\|\,q_{i}\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{K}w_{i}\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i}^{H}(v)\log\frac{p_{i}^{H}(v)}{q_{i}(v)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=\exp(\frac{-(i-1)}{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=\exp(\frac{-(i-1)}{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math> emphasizes the contribution of earlier positions. Because the teacher and <em id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> share the same architecture and prediction interface, their output distributions are naturally aligned despite the difference in historical information available to them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The history-aware teacher therefore serves as an optimization bridge between the autoregressive target model and the history-free drafter. Instead of forcing <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> to directly match a target distribution, the warm-up stage first exposes it to a structurally matched predictive distribution, providing a more stable starting point for subsequent target-model alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stable Stage.</span> Although the intermediate teacher facilitates optimization, the ultimate objective of <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> is to match the predictive distribution of the target model. We therefore discard the history-aware teacher
in the stable stage and directly optimize <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> against the target model using</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{stable}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{TV}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TV}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>stable</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{stable}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{TV}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TV}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes token-level cross-entropy and
<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TV}}=\sum_{i}w_{i}\|p_{i}^{T}-q_{i}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TV}}=\sum_{i}w_{i}\|p_{i}^{T}-q_{i}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> measures target–draft distribution discrepancy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
