<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and Baselines.</span> We evaluate <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> on Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B. We compare against vanilla autoregressive decoding (baseline) and representative speculative decoding methods, including EAGLE-3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2026)</a></cite>, DFlash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2026)</a></cite>, DFlash2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">inco.ai (2026)</a></cite>, DSpark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. (2026)</a></cite>, and DFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2026)</a></cite>. All serving experiments are conducted on NVIDIA H800 GPUs. The results of DFlash2 and DFlow are from RedHatAI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">RedHatAI (2026)</a></cite> or paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks.</span> We evaluate the performance in three different domains, general-purpose tasks, long-context tasks and agentic tasks. General-purpose tasks include three categories, Math: GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. (2021)</a></cite> and MATH-500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al. (2023)</a></cite>, Code: HumanEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a></cite>, MBPP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Austin et al. (2021)</a></cite>, and LiveCodeBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Chat: MT-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a></cite> and Alpaca <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Long-context tasks include Multi-Document QA, Long In-context Learning, Code Repository Understanding, and Long dialogue History in LongBench_v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Agentic tasks include AgentMemoryBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, AgentLongBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. (2026)</a></cite>, BFCL-v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al. (2024)</a></cite> and SWE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We use Open-PerfectBlend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite> which consists of 1.3 million samples containing chat, math, code and instruction-following data. We also collect 600K samples from NVIDIA Nemotron Post-Training Dataset V2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nathawani et al. (2025)</a></cite> to augment the dataset. We only use the prompts from the dataset and generate the response using the target models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> For all speculative decoding methods, we use a speculative block size of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=7</annotation></semantics></math> and follow the original implementation whenever available. <em id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> uses 12/14 layer target hidden states with sequence-wise concatenation, and a sequential-head rank of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=320/512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>320</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=320/512</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen3-4B/8B. We report average acceptance length <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and end-to-end decoding speedup over vanilla autoregressive decoding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Performance Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">General-purpose workloads.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports acceptance lengths on conventional reasoning, coding, and conversational benchmarks. <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> consistently matches or outperforms existing speculative drafters on both Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B. These results show that removing the historical draft KV cache does not sacrifice drafting quality on standard workloads, despite <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> operating only on target representations and a local anchor token.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Long-context workloads.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the advantage of <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> becomes more pronounced on LongBench v2, where it consistently improves both acceptance length and end-to-end speedup over DFlash and DSpark. This trend supports our central design motivation: as the context grows, conventional drafters increasingly rely on their own long-history modeling, whereas <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> delegates global-context modeling to the target model and performs only local prediction at the draft side.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic workloads.</span>
Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Performance Evaluation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the largest relative gains under agentic workloads. In particular, on SWE-Bench, <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> substantially improves acceptance length over DFlash and DSpark while achieving around <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> end-to-end speedup. These results indicate that history-free drafting is especially beneficial under the long, evolving contexts characteristic of agent execution</p>
</div>
