<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Ablation Study and Design Space Exploration</h3>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.23787v1/ablation.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/169;" width="548" height="169" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Ablation on training strategy and KV cache.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training strategy.</span> Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study and Design Space Exploration ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(a) compares our two-stage training strategy with direct single-stage training. The two-stage strategy consistently achieves higher acceptance length throughout the stable stage and improves the final performance. This result supports our motivation in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Two-stage Training Strategy ‣ 4 DLite Framework ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>: the history-aware isomorphic teacher provides an effective optimization bridge before direct alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Historical KV cache.</span> To examine whether historical draft states are necessary for accurate speculation, we additionally train two variants with sliding-window KV caches of 128 and 512 tokens using the same training data and setup as <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study and Design Space Exploration ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(b), after sufficient training, the three models achieve comparable acceptance lengths across the evaluated benchmarks. This suggests that historical KV states can facilitate prediction at earlier stages of training, but their advantage diminishes as the drafter learns to exploit contextual information transferred through the target hidden states. With sufficient training, <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> can therefore achieve comparable quality without explicitly maintaining KV cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design space exploration.</span> We further explore several architectural design choices of <em id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>, including the number of hidden states extracted from the target model, how multiple target hidden states are composed (feature-wise versus sequence-wise concatenation), and the rank of the lightweight sequential head. These dimensions jointly determine the trade-off between contextual representation capacity, drafting quality, and execution overhead. We use this design-space exploration in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Design Space exploration ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> to determine the final configurations reported in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
