<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>, a history-free speculative decoding framework for agentic AI. <em id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> removes the need for maintaining historical draft KV cache by directly reusing contextual hidden states from the target model and reformulating draft generation as local speculative prediction. On top of the history-free parallel backbone, a lightweight sequential head restores intra-block causal dependencies with low overhead, while a tailored two-stage training strategy addresses the optimization difficulty caused by the heterogeneous prediction structures of the draft and target models. Extensive experiments on general-purpose, long-context, and agentic workloads show that <em id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> achieves <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\sim 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>∼</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\sim 6</annotation></semantics></math> acceptance length and improves over the state-of-the-art semi-autoregressive drafter DSpark by up to <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math>, while eliminating historical draft KV-cache maintenance. These results demonstrate that removing redundant historical modeling from the drafter can simultaneously improve speculative decoding quality and system efficiency for emerging agentic workloads.</p>
</div>
