<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Training Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train <em id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> on the datasets described in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. For all training data, we only use the original prompts and regenerate responses using the corresponding target model, ensuring that the supervision is consistent with the target distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model configuration.</span> For Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B, the <em id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> backbone contains 5 Transformer layers with hidden dimension <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2560/4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2560</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2560/4096</annotation></semantics></math>. We extract 12/14 hidden states from the target model using sequence-wise composition. Specifically, we always retain the hidden states from the first and last layers, while uniformly sampling the remaining hidden states from the intermediate layers. The rank of lightweight sequential head is <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=320/512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>320</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=320/512</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen3-4B/8B, respectively. The speculative block length is set to <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=7</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training data.</span> Our training corpus contains approximately 1.9M samples in total, including 1.3M samples from Open-PerfectBlend and 600K samples from NVIDIA Nemotron Post-Training Dataset V2. We use only the prompts from these datasets and generate responses with the target model. The maximum sequence length is 4096, and each training sample contains 512 speculative blocks / anchors on average.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optimization.</span> We train <em id="A1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> using AdamW with a learning rate of <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and gradient clipping threshold 1.0. The global batch size is 32, with gradient accumulation of 1 step. We use cosine learning-rate decay with 0.04 warm-up ratio. Training is performed on 32 NVIDIA H800 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Two-stage training.</span> The positional weight decay <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 4. Training consists of a warm-up stage followed by a stable stage. In the warm-up stage, we first train the isomorphic history-aware teacher with the same data mentioned above using token-level cross-entropy for 8 epochs. We then train <em id="A1.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> against this teacher using forward KL divergence for 2 epochs. In the stable stage, the history-aware teacher is removed and <em id="A1.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> is directly optimized against the target model using</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{stable}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{TV}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TV}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>stable</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{stable}}=\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}+\lambda_{\mathrm{TV}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TV}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{CE}}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{TV}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{TV}}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The stable stage lasts for 6 epochs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Design Space exploration</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a design space exploration on Qwen3-8B to study three key architectural choices of <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>: (1) the number of target hidden states used as contextual features, (2) the composition strategy for multiple target hidden states, and (3) the rank of the lightweight sequential head. To reduce the cost of design exploration, all variants in this section are trained on the same 80K-sample subset, while keeping the remaining training configurations unchanged. We evaluate the average acceptance length on Alpaca, GSM8K, MBPP, and Multi-Document QA, covering conversational, mathematical, coding, and long-context workloads.</p>
</div>
