<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of target hidden states.</span> Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.2 Design Space exploration ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that increasing the number of target hidden states initially leads to clear improvements in acceptance length. The gains, however, quickly saturate: increasing the number from 7 to 14 improves performance consistently across all four workloads, while using 20 or 36 hidden states provides little or no additional benefit. This suggests that a moderate number of intermediate target representations is sufficient to capture complementary contextual information, while further increasing the number of features introduces redundant representations and additional computation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hidden-state composition.</span> We further compare two ways of combining hidden states extracted from different target layers. Sequence-wise concatenation treats representations from different layers as separate input elements, while feature-wise concatenation concatenates them along the hidden dimension. Sequence-wise concatenation consistently outperforms feature-wise concatenation across all evaluated workloads, increasing the average acceptance length from 3.04 to 3.30. We therefore adopt sequence-wise composition in <em id="A1.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em>. This result also supports the use of depth embeddings in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 History-free semi-autoregressive Architecture ‣ 4 DLite Framework ‣ DLite: History-Free Speculative Decoding for Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, which explicitly identify the source layers of hidden states when they are presented as separate inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential-head rank.</span> Finally, we vary the rank of the lightweight sequential head from 1/16 of the hidden dimension (256) to the full hidden dimension (4096). Increasing the rank from 256 to 512 yields a noticeable improvement in drafting quality while increasing the end-to-end latency by only 0.3%, whereas larger ranks provide only marginal gains while introducing substantial computational overhead. This suggests that the information required by the draft model can be sufficiently resolved in a low-rank space. For example, the average acceptance length increases from 3.16 at rank 256 to 3.30 at rank 512, but only reaches 3.33 with the full 4096-dimensional head. Considering the additional sequential computation introduced by a larger rank and the diminishing performance gains, we choose a relatively small rank to balance drafting quality and execution efficiency.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation details.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <em id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DLite</em> on four agent-oriented benchmarks using Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B targets. For SWE-Bench, we use 50 prompts from 64K settings (40K–64K input tokens). For AgentLongBench, we use 50 prompts from the 64K setting (55.5K–74.1K input tokens). For MemoryAgentBench, we select 100 FactConsolidation, 50 EventQA, and 30 free-form DetectiveQA questions (66K–118K input tokens), constructing one independent long-context prompt per question. For BFCL, we use 41 sessions from the <span id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">multi_turn_long_context</span> category, run its multi-turn tool-execution harness through a local OpenAI-compatible inference adapter, and aggregate model calls whose input exceeds 32K tokens. BFCL tool-execution time is excluded.</p>
</div>
